<BaseLayout title="AI Systems Reliability Lab — Evaluation & Failure Forensics" description="A focused technical-proof program pairing frozen AI release evaluation with bounded failure forensics, structured traces, and explicit human evidence authority.">
  <article class="lab-case-study">
    <header class="lab-hero shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Focused technical proof · Reliability Lab</p>
      <h1>Evaluate the release.<br />Diagnose the failure.</h1>
      <p class="lab-subtitle">A focused program of bounded AI reliability experiments: first asking whether a candidate configuration earns release, then requiring evidence of where a bad outcome actually began.</p>
      <p class="lab-summary">The Reliability Lab makes system judgment inspectable. Lab 01 tests frozen release evaluation and the authority of its evaluator. Lab 02 traces failure through real stage-to-stage state consumption, separating a root cause from the downstream symptom that made it visible.</p>
      <p class="lab-cta"><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/matt-rfs/ai-systems-reliability-lab">View the evidence on GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><span aria-hidden="true"> · </span><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/matt-rfs/matthew-ralph-work-samples/tree/main/ai-systems-reliability-lab">View curated work sample <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></p>
      <aside class="authority-ledger authority-ledger-lab" aria-label="AI Systems Reliability Lab authority ledger"><p class="eyebrow">Authority ledger</p><div><section><strong>System did</strong><span>Evaluate configurations and trace failure lineage.</span></section><section><strong>Matthew directed</strong><span>Experiment design, release criteria, and causal evidence requirements.</span></section><section><strong>Human authority remained</strong><span>Release judgment, evaluator authority, and publication acceptance.</span></section></div></aside>
    </header>

    <section class="lab-section shell" aria-labelledby="lab-purpose"><div class="lab-label"><p class="eyebrow">01 · Proof map</p></div><div class="lab-body"><h2 id="lab-purpose">Two bounded questions. One evidence standard.</h2><div class="lab-method-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Lab 01 · Evaluate</p><strong>Model Regression Gate</strong><span>Should a candidate AI configuration be released?</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Lab 02 · Diagnose</p><strong>Failure Forensics</strong><span>Where did a bad outcome actually begin?</span></div></div><p class="lab-note">The program moves from <strong>evaluate → diagnose</strong>: evidence must establish both whether a configuration earned release and where a failure actually started.</p></div></section>

    <section class="lab-section shell" aria-labelledby="lab-one"><div class="lab-label"><p class="eyebrow">02 · Lab 01</p></div><div class="lab-body"><h2 id="lab-one">A frozen release decision remained visible after the evaluator was audited.</h2><p>Lab 01 compared two frozen configurations across 30 synthetic cases. Both passed deterministic validation, but the historical automated release decision was <strong>FAIL</strong>: the candidate’s 58.6% factual-support score remained below the frozen 90% threshold.</p><div class="lab-method-grid"><div><strong>Frozen experiment</strong><span>30 cases · both configurations 30 / 30 deterministic checks · 0 regressions.</span></div><div><strong>Human audit</strong><span>60 / 60 outputs reviewed. The candidate was materially weaker, and the local semantic evaluator had not earned release authority.</span></div></div><table class="lab-comparison"><caption>Lab 01 human-audit comparison</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Measure</th><th scope="col">Baseline</th><th scope="col">Candidate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Human-confirmed factual support</th><td>96.7%</td><td>70.0%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Judge / human agreement</th><td>53.3%</td><td>43.3%</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="lab-note">Human review added interpretation without rewriting the historical automated FAIL.</p></div></section>

    <section class="lab-section shell" aria-labelledby="lab-two"><div class="lab-label"><p class="eyebrow">03 · Lab 02</p></div><div class="lab-body"><h2 id="lab-two">A final bad brief may be a downstream symptom—not the root cause.</h2><p>Lab 02 is a frozen synthetic fixture with five stages: <strong>Normalize → Classify → Select evidence → Recommend → Generate final brief</strong>. It records inspectable intermediate state and structured traces so the first causal failure can be distinguished from where the outcome became visible.</p></div></section>

    <section class="lab-ledger shell" aria-label="Lab 02 evidence at a glance">
      <article><p class="eyebrow">Frozen fixture</p><strong>14 cases</strong><span>3 clean · 11 injected failures</span></article>
      <article><p class="eyebrow">Causal localization</p><strong>14 / 14</strong><span>Expected first stages, bounded to this fixture</span></article>
      <article><p class="eyebrow">Trace completeness</p><strong>70 / 70</strong><span>Structured stage traces complete</span></article>
      <article><p class="eyebrow">Causal outcomes</p><strong>4 · 5 · 5</strong><span>Propagated · contained · local/no downstream propagation</span></article>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-section shell" aria-labelledby="lab-causality"><div class="lab-label"><p class="eyebrow">04 · Causality</p></div><div class="lab-body"><h2 id="lab-causality">The stage where a problem appears is not necessarily the stage that caused it.</h2><ol class="lab-decision-chain"><li><strong>Propagated</strong><span>An earlier invalid state was actually consumed by a later stage and observed in its output.</span></li><li><strong>Contained</strong><span>A failed stage stopped later work fail-closed; blocked stages were not treated as propagated failures.</span></li><li><strong>Local</strong><span>The failure had no invalid downstream output.</span></li><li><strong>Root cause</strong><span>A classification defect can produce evidence, recommendation, and final-brief symptoms; final generation is not automatically the cause.</span></li></ol><p class="lab-note">The canonical <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/matt-rfs/ai-systems-reliability-lab/blob/main/labs/02-failure-forensics/results/failure_lineage.svg">failure-lineage visual <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a> shows this distinction in the technical evidence record.</p></div></section>

    <section class="lab-section shell" aria-labelledby="lab-judgment"><div class="lab-label"><p class="eyebrow">05 · Human judgment</p></div><div class="lab-body"><h2 id="lab-judgment">Mechanically plausible evidence did not earn publication authority.</h2><p>Matthew designed the forensic experiment around causal failure localization, required inspectable intermediate state and real stage-to-stage consumption, rejected a mechanically plausible but causally weak first publication candidate, and directed the repaired experiment and validation against stricter evidence requirements.</p><div class="lab-authorship"><div><strong>Causality, not sequence</strong><span>Stage order alone did not prove propagation; actual downstream consumption had to be observed.</span></div><div><strong>Containment, not propagation</strong><span>Fail-closed blocking was distinguished from an invalid state that had travelled downstream.</span></div><div><strong>Repair direction</strong><span>The experiment was repaired to consume real accumulated stage state rather than assign propagation mechanically.</span></div><div><strong>Regression preservation</strong><span>`evidence-01` fails at evidence selection before its bounded correction and passes after it; the loop remains a regression test.</span></div></div></div></section>

    <section class="lab-section shell" aria-labelledby="lab-boundary"><div class="lab-label"><p class="eyebrow">06 · Boundaries</p></div><div class="lab-body"><h2 id="lab-boundary">Small, inspectable experiments—not a production claim.</h2><div class="lab-boundaries"><div><p class="eyebrow">What this demonstrates</p><ul><li>Frozen evaluation and deterministic failure analysis</li><li>Structured traces and bounded causal localization</li><li>Evidence integrity and explicit human authority</li></ul></div><div><p class="eyebrow">What this does not claim</p><ul><li>Production incident forensics or observability ownership</li><li>Live model monitoring or autonomous remediation</li><li>Generalized diagnostic accuracy or enterprise-scale operation</li></ul></div></div><p class="lab-note">Lab 02 is synthetic, deterministic, and local. It invokes no model, reports token fields as unavailable, and incurred $0 API/service spend. Its 1.656 ms measurement is fixture execution timing—not model inference or production latency.</p></div></section>

    <section class="lab-evidence shell" aria-labelledby="lab-evidence"><p class="eyebrow">07 · Inspect evidence</p><h2 id="lab-evidence">Start with the curated interpretation. Go deeper when needed.</h2><p>The curated work sample makes the program easy to enter; the canonical public repository remains the technical source of truth for both Labs.</p><p><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/matt-rfs/matthew-ralph-work-samples/tree/main/ai-systems-reliability-lab">Open curated Reliability Lab work sample <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><span aria-hidden="true"> · </span><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/matt-rfs/ai-systems-reliability-lab/blob/main/labs/02-failure-forensics/README.md">Open Lab 02 technical evidence <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></p></section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
